.TH SLEEP 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
sleep \- pause for a specified time
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sleep
.IR number [ suffix ]...
.br
.B sleep inf
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sleep
waits for the total of the times that its arguments give, then exits.
It reads no input and writes no output.
This
.B sleep
is a busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
Each argument is a
.I number
of seconds, or of another unit named by the optional
.IR suffix :
.TP
.B s
Seconds, the default.
.TP
.B m
Minutes, which are 60 seconds.
.TP
.B h
Hours, which are 3600 seconds.
.TP
.B d
Days, which are 86400 seconds.
.PP
The suffix is one lowercase letter that follows the number directly.
The number is a string of decimal digits with at most one period, as in
.BR 90 ,
.BR 1.5 ,
or
.BR .5 .
A period may lack digits on one side, as in
.BR 5.\& ,
but not on both.
Nothing else is part of a number: no sign, exponent, hexadecimal
digits, white space, or comma.
A number with no period must not exceed 4294967295 once its suffix is
applied.
A number with a period need only fit in a double, but see
.BR NOTES .
.PP
The delay is the sum of the arguments, rounded down to whole
milliseconds.
All arguments are checked before the wait begins, so an invalid argument
anywhere means no delay at all.
A total of zero returns at once.
.PP
If the first argument begins with
.BR inf ,
in any mix of uppercase and lowercase, such as
.BR inf ,
.BR INFINITY ,
or
.BR information ,
.B sleep
never returns on its own.
The other arguments are then not examined.
.SH OPTIONS
.B sleep
has no options.
A first argument of
.B \-\-
is skipped, and every other argument that begins with
.B \-
is an invalid number, since numbers cannot be negative.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument, and any further arguments
are then ignored.
In any other position it is an invalid number.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B sleep
reads no environment variables.
The startup code shared by all applets reads
.BR BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS ,
.BR BB_GLOBBING ,
and
.BR BB_UMASK ,
and may trim a trailing semicolon from
.BR PATH .
None of these affects how
.B sleep
waits.
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The delay elapsed, or the first argument was
.BR \-\-help .
.TP
.B 1
There were no arguments, or an argument was invalid.
Nothing is waited for.
.PP
An argument without a period that is not a number, or whose digits
exceed 4294967295, is reported on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sleep: invalid number \(aq\fIargument\fR\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
and one that exceeds it only after its suffix is applied, as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
sleep: number \fIargument\fR is not in 0..4294967295 range
.EE
.RE
.PP
No arguments, or only
.BR \-\- ,
give a usage message on standard error.
So does an argument with a period whose leading digits and periods are
not a number that fits in a double, such as a lone
.B .\&
or
.BR 1.2.3 .
.SH EXAMPLES
Pause for half a second:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sleep 0.5
.EE
.RE
.PP
Pause for an hour and a half, in either of two ways:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ sleep 1.5h
$ sleep 1h 30m
.EE
.RE
.PP
Wait for a file to appear:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ until test \-e ready.txt; do sleep 1; done
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print a message ten seconds from now, without waiting for it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ (sleep 10; echo time is up) &
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR sleep :
.IP \(bu 2
Numbers are checked strictly, as described above.
.IP \(bu 2
There is no
.BR \-\-version ,
and
.B \-\-help
is recognized only as the first argument.
.IP \(bu 2
Only the first argument can mean infinity, and it does so when it
merely begins with
.BR inf .
.IP \(bu 2
A number with no period has an upper limit.
.PP
The delay is passed to the Windows
.B Sleep
function in milliseconds, and busybox does not check it against the
32-bit limit of that function.
The total therefore wraps around modulo 4294967296 milliseconds, which
is 49 days, 17 hours, 2 minutes, and 47.296 seconds.
A total of 4294968 seconds waits for 0.7 seconds, and
.B 50d
for about 7 hours.
A total of 4294967295 milliseconds after wrapping, such as
.BR 4294967.2955 ,
is the value that
.B Sleep
takes to mean forever, so it never returns.
.PP
The invalid number message for an argument that has a period quotes a
modified copy of it, in which the leading digits and periods are
replaced by a single
.BR 1 .
So
.B 1.5e3
is reported as
.BR 1e3 .
An argument with a period but no leading digit or period, such as
.BR \-1.5 ,
gets a
.B 1
put in front instead, and is reported as
.BR 1\-1.5 .
.PP
Ctrl+C ends
.B sleep
at once.
The kit's
.BR sh (1)
runs an applet that busybox marks
.BR NOFORK ,
such as
.BR usleep (1),
inside its own process, where Ctrl+C takes effect only after the applet
returns, but
.B sleep
is not such an applet.
The shell reports status 130 for a
.B sleep
ended by Ctrl+C, and 143 for one ended by
.BR kill (1)
with its default signal.
Run outside the shell, as
.B busybox sleep
or through the launcher
.IR sleep.exe ,
it exits with status
.B 0xC000013A
on Ctrl+C.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR kill (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR timeout (1),
.BR usleep (1),
.BR watch (1)
